WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.427
Molecules have shapes, hands, and moves.

2
00:00:03.527 --> 00:00:05.067
Let's watch them in motion.

3
00:00:09.799 --> 00:00:19.968
Chemistry is still being rewritten. In heavy atoms like bismuth, electrons move so fast that Einstein's relativity reshapes the chemical bond itself.

4
00:00:20.668 --> 00:00:27.709
And in 2026, chemists finally built boron's own buckyball: a hollow cage of 80 atoms.

5
00:00:28.509 --> 00:00:37.401
Why do molecules have shapes at all? Electron pairs repel. Shake methane's four pairs, and they spread out into a perfect tetrahedron.

6
00:00:38.099 --> 00:00:46.167
Water has two lone pairs. They push harder, squeezing the angle to 104.5°, and that's why water is bent.

7
00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:51.681
Sulfur hexafluoride spreads six bonds to the corners of an octahedron.

8
00:00:52.481 --> 00:00:59.763
Some molecules come in left- and right-handed forms: mirror images that can never be lined up, just like your hands.

9
00:01:00.462 --> 00:01:06.069
Your body can tell them apart. A receptor shaped for one hand simply won't fit the other.

10
00:01:06.769 --> 00:01:13.774
Chemists name them R or S, by ranking the groups and following them around: clockwise, or anticlockwise.

11
00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:26.209
Metal–organic frameworks are crystals that are mostly empty space. Raise the pressure, and their pores soak up carbon dioxide. They won the 2025 Nobel Prize.

12
00:01:27.209 --> 00:01:38.287
Reactions are electrons on the move. In an SN2 reaction, hydroxide attacks from behind, and the molecule flips inside out, like an umbrella in the wind.

13
00:01:39.087 --> 00:01:46.428
In SN1, the leaving group goes first. That leaves a flat carbocation, which can be attacked from either side.

14
00:01:47.228 --> 00:01:54.852
In a Diels–Alder reaction, six electrons swing around a ring all at once, stitching two molecules into a brand-new ring.

15
00:01:55.853 --> 00:02:05.171
Every reaction has a hill to climb: the activation energy. An exothermic reaction ends lower than it starts, and the difference comes out as heat.

16
00:02:05.971 --> 00:02:13.741
A catalyst offers a lower hill. Suddenly far more molecules make it over the top, and the reaction speeds up enormously.

17
00:02:17.266 --> 00:02:19.763
Electron pairs set a molecule's shape.

18
00:02:19.863 --> 00:02:22.303
Some molecules come in mirror-image pairs.

19
00:02:22.403 --> 00:02:24.753
And reactions are electrons on the move.

20
00:02:24.666 --> 00:02:26.487
Next time: reading light.
